INT. ARMORED CAR DEPOT - LOADING BAY - EARLY MORNING

A concrete bay. A steel truck backed to the dock. Fluorescents hum.

CORRIE (40s, depot supervisor, clip-on tie) signs a form on a clipboard. BASIL (60s, driver, heavy belt, thermos) leans on the truck's fender. DUKE (30s, loader, gloves, neck tattoo) wheels a cart of canvas sacks toward the cargo door.

A DELIVERY MAN (30s, brown uniform, cap low, hand truck) enters through the side gate, pushing a dolly stacked with water jugs.

DELIVERY MAN
Water for the break room.

CORRIE
We didn't order water.

DELIVERY MAN
It's a service, ma'am. Free, until the end of the month.

CORRIE
Leave it by the door.

He parks the dolly. Basil sips from his thermos. Duke lifts a sack, grunts.

BASIL
Heavy day.

DUKE
Always is.

The Delivery Man glances at the truck, then at the clock. 6:58.

WINSTON
(softly, to himself)
Two minutes.

CORRIE
Pardon?

WINSTON
I said, two minutes until my shift ends.

He tugs the bottom jug. A hidden latch clicks. Smoke hisses out of the stack.

BASIL
What the...

Duke drops the sack. Corrie backs up. Winston pulls a mask from under his jacket, snaps it over his face.

WINSTON
Nobody move.

The bay door slams down behind them.
